1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,260
Boston.

2
00:00:01,580 --> 00:00:04,430
January fifteenth, nineteen nineteen.

3
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:10,690
It is lunchtime in the North End, and for the first time in weeks, the weather is almost pleasant.

4
00:00:11,708 --> 00:00:18,398
Then, at about twelve thirty, a wall of something dark and shining comes rolling down Commercial Street.

5
00:00:18,718 --> 00:00:21,238
It is taller than a two-story house.

6
00:00:21,558 --> 00:00:24,568
It is moving faster than a man can run.

7
00:00:24,888 --> 00:00:26,518
And it is not water.

8
00:00:27,539 --> 00:00:29,159
It is molasses.

9
00:00:29,479 --> 00:00:31,869
More than two million gallons of it.

10
00:00:32,189 --> 00:00:36,839
By nightfall, twenty-one people are dead and a hundred and fifty are hurt.

11
00:00:37,159 --> 00:00:39,029
So here is the question.

12
00:00:39,349 --> 00:00:41,399
How does syrup kill people?

13
00:00:41,849 --> 00:00:46,689
And why did the answer change the way America builds almost everything?

14
00:00:47,842 --> 00:00:55,432
To find out, we have to go back four years, to a steel tank by the harbor, and to a man who was in a very big hurry.

15
00:00:59,257 --> 00:01:03,167
First, why was there so much molasses in Boston at all?

16
00:01:03,617 --> 00:01:09,617
Molasses is the thick, dark syrup left over when sugar cane is boiled into sugar.

17
00:01:09,937 --> 00:01:16,337
Ships carried it up from the Caribbean, and New England had been turning it into rum for two hundred years.

18
00:01:17,353 --> 00:01:23,333
But in nineteen fifteen, the most valuable thing you could make from molasses was not rum.

19
00:01:23,653 --> 00:01:26,963
Ferment it, and you get industrial alcohol.

20
00:01:27,283 --> 00:01:32,163
And industrial alcohol was a key ingredient in making explosives.

21
00:01:32,483 --> 00:01:37,963
Europe was at war, and explosives were selling as fast as factories could make them.

22
00:01:38,986 --> 00:01:50,416
A company called Purity Distilling, which was soon bought by the United States Industrial Alcohol Company, wanted a giant storage tank right on the Boston waterfront.

23
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:53,066
Ships would pump molasses into it.

24
00:01:53,386 --> 00:01:58,456
Then a pipeline would carry it to a distillery across the river in Cambridge.

25
00:02:02,276 --> 00:02:06,196
The man in charge of building that tank was Arthur Jell.

26
00:02:06,516 --> 00:02:08,496
He was the company's treasurer.

27
00:02:08,816 --> 00:02:10,846
He was not an engineer.

28
00:02:11,166 --> 00:02:13,126
He could not read a blueprint.

29
00:02:13,446 --> 00:02:18,416
And he was under pressure to get the tank finished before the next shipment arrived.

30
00:02:19,435 --> 00:02:23,285
The tank went up on Commercial Street in nineteen fifteen.

31
00:02:23,605 --> 00:02:30,215
It was fifty feet tall and ninety feet wide, and it could hold about two point three million gallons.

32
00:02:30,535 --> 00:02:38,875
It sat in one of the most crowded neighborhoods in the city, beside homes, a firehouse, a playground, and the elevated railway.

33
00:02:39,893 --> 00:02:46,143
A new tank like this should be tested by filling it to the top with water, to see whether it leaks.

34
00:02:46,463 --> 00:02:47,883
Jell skipped that.

35
00:02:48,203 --> 00:02:55,253
He ran only a few inches of water into the bottom, declared it fine, and ordered the first load of molasses.

36
00:02:56,268 --> 00:02:59,058
The tank leaked from the very first day.

37
00:02:59,378 --> 00:03:03,568
Thin brown streaks ran down the seams between the steel plates.

38
00:03:03,888 --> 00:03:09,608
Neighborhood families brought pots and cans and collected the dripping syrup to take home.

39
00:03:10,624 --> 00:03:12,874
People also noticed the noise.

40
00:03:13,194 --> 00:03:16,924
Every time the tank was filled, it groaned and rumbled.

41
00:03:17,244 --> 00:03:18,804
Workers complained.

42
00:03:19,124 --> 00:03:22,814
One of them even brought Jell a piece of flaking steel.

43
00:03:23,134 --> 00:03:26,264
Jell's response was not to fix the tank.

44
00:03:26,584 --> 00:03:31,684
His response was to have it painted brown, so the leaks would be harder to see.

45
00:03:32,701 --> 00:03:35,421
And there was one more pressure on the tank.

46
00:03:35,741 --> 00:03:43,261
By January nineteen nineteen, the country was days away from writing a ban on alcohol into the Constitution.

47
00:03:43,581 --> 00:03:47,621
The movement for Prohibition was winning state after state.

48
00:03:47,941 --> 00:03:56,641
Several historians argue the company was racing to turn as much molasses into alcohol as it could before the law arrived.

49
00:04:00,459 --> 00:04:02,749
Now, back to January fifteenth.

50
00:04:03,069 --> 00:04:07,629
For days, Boston had been bitterly cold, close to zero degrees.

51
00:04:07,949 --> 00:04:12,049
Then, almost overnight, the temperature jumped to over forty.

52
00:04:12,369 --> 00:04:19,759
Just days before, a ship had delivered a fresh load of warm molasses, heated so that it would pump more easily.

53
00:04:20,079 --> 00:04:24,169
It was poured on top of the cold molasses already inside.

54
00:04:24,489 --> 00:04:26,449
The tank was almost full.

55
00:04:27,468 --> 00:04:30,558
Around noon, the North End went about its day.

56
00:04:30,878 --> 00:04:33,698
Children were walking home from school for lunch.

57
00:04:34,018 --> 00:04:37,738
Men from the city paving yard were eating in their shed.

58
00:04:38,058 --> 00:04:41,848
Firefighters at Engine thirty-one were playing cards.

59
00:04:42,168 --> 00:04:46,178
A streetcar rattled along the elevated tracks overhead.

60
00:04:49,999 --> 00:04:52,929
Then, at about twelve thirty, people heard it.

61
00:04:53,249 --> 00:04:55,689
Some said it sounded like thunder.

62
00:04:56,009 --> 00:04:59,039
Others said it sounded like a machine gun.

63
00:04:59,359 --> 00:05:03,139
It was the rivets, popping out of the tank one after another.

64
00:05:03,459 --> 00:05:12,509
The steel walls split open, and two point three million gallons of molasses, weighing about twelve thousand tons, came out all at once.

65
00:05:13,526 --> 00:05:15,636
What came out was a wave.

66
00:05:15,956 --> 00:05:22,446
At its peak it was around twenty-five feet high, and it moved at about thirty-five miles an hour.

67
00:05:22,766 --> 00:05:25,136
It flattened the company's office.

68
00:05:25,456 --> 00:05:27,356
It smashed the paving yard.

69
00:05:27,676 --> 00:05:35,416
A sheet of the tank's steel slammed into the elevated railway and buckled its supports, just after a train had passed.

70
00:05:36,439 --> 00:05:39,959
The wave knocked the firehouse off its foundation.

71
00:05:40,279 --> 00:05:42,749
It lifted houses and crushed them.

72
00:05:43,069 --> 00:05:45,749
A truck was thrown into the harbor.

73
00:05:46,069 --> 00:05:50,589
Blocks of the neighborhood were flooded two to three feet deep in syrup.

74
00:05:51,605 --> 00:05:56,215
A boy named Anthony di Stasio was walking home with his sisters.

75
00:05:56,535 --> 00:06:01,825
The wave picked him up and carried him along on its crest, almost like he was surfing.

76
00:06:02,145 --> 00:06:05,345
Then it rolled him over and over and dropped him.

77
00:06:05,665 --> 00:06:06,865
He survived.

78
00:06:07,185 --> 00:06:10,315
His ten-year-old sister Maria did not.

79
00:06:11,334 --> 00:06:13,134
So, how does syrup kill?

80
00:06:13,584 --> 00:06:15,854
The answer is in the physics.

81
00:06:16,174 --> 00:06:22,194
Molasses is about forty percent heavier than water, so the wave hit with terrible force.

82
00:06:22,514 --> 00:06:25,464
And molasses changes how thick it is.

83
00:06:25,784 --> 00:06:28,924
When it is warm and moving fast, it flows.

84
00:06:29,244 --> 00:06:33,594
When it cools down, it turns thick and sticky, almost like glue.

85
00:06:34,616 --> 00:06:36,846
That is exactly what happened.

86
00:06:37,166 --> 00:06:46,846
In two thousand sixteen, a team at Harvard studied the disaster using old maps, weather records, and a scale model flooded with corn syrup.

87
00:06:47,166 --> 00:06:53,466
They found that the warm molasses rushed out fast, then cooled quickly in the January air.

88
00:06:53,786 --> 00:06:58,046
Within minutes, the people and horses caught in it could barely move.

89
00:06:58,366 --> 00:07:01,956
Many of the victims did not die from the impact.

90
00:07:02,276 --> 00:07:05,256
They suffocated, trapped in the sticky mass.

91
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:17,890
The first rescuers to arrive were a hundred and sixteen young cadets from the training ship U S S Nantucket, docked nearby.

92
00:07:18,210 --> 00:07:24,170
They ran toward the disaster and waded into the syrup, pulling out anyone they could reach.

93
00:07:24,490 --> 00:07:28,930
Police, Red Cross nurses, soldiers and sailors soon followed.

94
00:07:29,250 --> 00:07:33,600
Doctors set up an emergency hospital in a nearby building.

95
00:07:34,624 --> 00:07:36,834
The search went on for four days.

96
00:07:37,154 --> 00:07:42,294
Some victims were so coated in molasses that they were hard to recognize.

97
00:07:42,614 --> 00:07:46,744
Others had been swept into the harbor and were not found for months.

98
00:07:47,064 --> 00:07:53,554
Among the twenty-one dead were laborers, teamsters, a firefighter, and two ten-year-old children.

99
00:07:54,572 --> 00:07:56,282
Then came the cleanup.

100
00:07:56,602 --> 00:08:02,962
Crews sprayed the streets with salt water from fireboats and scattered sand to soak up the syrup.

101
00:08:03,282 --> 00:08:10,152
Rescuers and sightseers tracked it everywhere: onto streetcars, into homes, onto telephones.

102
00:08:10,472 --> 00:08:14,972
As one account put it, everything a Bostonian touched was sticky.

103
00:08:15,292 --> 00:08:17,942
The harbor stayed brown until summer.

104
00:08:21,765 --> 00:08:24,665
Now the question became, who was to blame?

105
00:08:25,115 --> 00:08:27,605
The company had an answer ready.

106
00:08:27,925 --> 00:08:30,535
It said the tank had not failed at all.

107
00:08:30,855 --> 00:08:34,045
It said anarchists had blown it up with a bomb.

108
00:08:34,365 --> 00:08:41,165
After all, the alcohol went into explosives, and bombings by radicals were real fears at the time.

109
00:08:42,184 --> 00:08:44,744
But the families did not accept that.

110
00:08:45,064 --> 00:08:52,694
More than a hundred people sued the company together, in one of the first class-action lawsuits in Massachusetts history.

111
00:08:53,014 --> 00:08:57,084
The court appointed an auditor, Hugh Ogden, to hear the evidence.

112
00:08:57,404 --> 00:09:03,824
The hearings went on for about three years, with a mountain of testimony from witnesses and experts.

113
00:09:04,848 --> 00:09:08,328
In nineteen twenty-five, Ogden gave his verdict.

114
00:09:08,648 --> 00:09:10,098
There was no bomb.

115
00:09:10,418 --> 00:09:16,108
The tank had simply been too weak for the load it carried, and the company was responsible.

116
00:09:16,428 --> 00:09:23,598
It paid about six hundred and twenty-eight thousand dollars in damages, more than eleven million dollars today.

117
00:09:24,618 --> 00:09:27,848
Later investigations found even more.

118
00:09:28,168 --> 00:09:38,338
A two thousand fourteen analysis concluded that the steel walls were only about half as thick as they should have been, even by the standards of the time.

119
00:09:38,658 --> 00:09:43,708
The steel was also low in manganese, which made it more brittle in the cold.

120
00:09:44,028 --> 00:09:46,728
The cracks began at the rivet holes.

121
00:09:50,549 --> 00:09:52,909
And that brings us back to our question.

122
00:09:53,229 --> 00:09:55,929
How did syrup kill twenty-one people?

123
00:09:56,379 --> 00:09:59,229
Not by bad luck, and not by a bomb.

124
00:09:59,549 --> 00:10:07,739
It was a giant tank, built by a man who was not an engineer, tested by no one, and painted brown to hide the warnings.

125
00:10:08,059 --> 00:10:14,429
So Massachusetts and other states began to require something that sounds obvious today.

126
00:10:14,749 --> 00:10:23,149
Before a big structure is built, a licensed engineer or architect must check the calculations and sign their name to them.

127
00:10:23,469 --> 00:10:27,269
That kind of signature is now standard across the country.

128
00:10:27,589 --> 00:10:32,729
Every bridge, tower, and tank you walk past today depends on that idea.

129
00:10:33,753 --> 00:10:36,163
And the race against Prohibition?

130
00:10:36,613 --> 00:10:45,783
On January sixteenth, nineteen nineteen, the very next day, Nebraska became the thirty-sixth state to ratify the Eighteenth Amendment.

131
00:10:46,103 --> 00:10:49,583
Prohibition was now part of the Constitution.

132
00:10:49,903 --> 00:10:55,483
The company had been racing a deadline that arrived one day after its tank collapsed.

133
00:10:56,499 --> 00:11:01,809
Today, the spot where the tank stood is a park with a Little League baseball field.

134
00:11:02,129 --> 00:11:07,039
Its concrete base is still there, about twenty inches under the infield.

135
00:11:07,359 --> 00:11:14,259
For decades, people in the North End said that on hot summer days, you could still smell molasses in the air.

136
00:11:15,283 --> 00:11:20,933
Twenty-one people died that day because someone decided a warning was a paint job.

137
00:11:21,253 --> 00:11:29,563
So next time you see a crack in something that is supposed to be strong, remember Boston, and remember the wave that wasn't water.

138
00:11:29,883 --> 00:11:38,903
If you want more forgotten stories from American history, subscribe, and tell us in the comments which disaster we should cover next.

